Up there in Minnesota. the town is spelled ELY.

21
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
I mistakenly pronounced it Eli a couple of podcasts ago,

22
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
And then Ann was kind enough to correct my pronunciation of it

23
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
And last week I had it as Ellie,

24
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
And you know what

25
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
She says: this: hi again, Clark.

26
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:21,000
It was a great podcast, but it appears I didn't describe the pronunciation of ELY well enough, though

27
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Ellie is pronounced with two long E sounds.

28
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
So it's Eli, Eli.

29
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Okay,

30
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:30,000
So now we got it.

31
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Thanks again, Ann.

32
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:33,000
It's those darn flatlanders again.

33
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,000
You know what I'm saying.

34
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Larry Geiger got in touch and said podcast 134, one of the best ones ever.

35
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
I was sitting in a house 16 miles from pad A watching the video feed from the moon

36
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:49,000
on our old RCA TV set when Armstrong said those famous words.

37
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
We had just watched the launch of Apollo 11 a few days earlier. while standing down the street

38
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
looking across the Banana River,

39
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:04,000
My dad was in the LLC manning the video switcher for the cameras on the pad during the launch.

40
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
It was an amazing time.

41
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,000
And the other feature on last week's podcast: you talked about a young scout slowly gaining independence from his dad,

42
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
but you didn't tell the other side of the story.

43
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
I was red faced, tired and sweaty and sore, headed up the last couple of hundred feet to the evening shelter on the Appalachian Trail

44
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
When who comes around the corner but my son and his best friend Carl?

45
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
he hiked back down the trail to check on his dad and make sure everything was fine.

46
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,000
He offered to take my pack the rest of the way, but I carried it in camp.

47
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,000
He helped me set up my tent and we joined the other guys around the fire in front of the shelter for dinner.

48
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
A few years later, on a Wednesday, after four days on the Appalachian Trail,

49
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
he drove me all the way home from North Carolina to Central Florida

50
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
when the base camper came to tell us that my dad probably wasn't going to make it through the day

51
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
and my wife and mom wanted me to come home.

52
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:56,000
My son and I have shared many special moments like this at sea base

53
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,000
and on our AT hikes and canoe trips and camping

54
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
And, as you said, if we trust the program and go with it,

55
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:07,000
we're going to get to know our sons a lot better and they're going to get to know us

56
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:11,000
Well. thank you so much, Larry. I certainly appreciate that

57
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
This week on the podcast. just a couple of quick things.

58
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:16,000
It's Labor Day, for one thing,

59
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
So you know we're kind of taking it easy,

60
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
but I have a couple of things for you in Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

61
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
One is 10 ways to work with your senior patrol leader.

62
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:30,000
And the second one asks the question: why do scouts drop out of scouting?

63
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Two things that'll take us into the fall:

64
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Lots of camping going on, lots of meetings, things like that.

65
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,000
I'm looking forward to it. I know you are,

66
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:38,000
So let's get started, shall we?

67
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,000
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

68
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Senior patrol leader: that's the key leadership position.

69
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Now, how do we work out the relationship that we have with our senior patrol?

70
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
You're the scout master. You're supposed to be watching what's going on.

71
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
You're supposed to mentor this young man through the process of learning to lead.

72
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Well, how does that happen? I've got 10 points for you.

73
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
One is to let him own the program.

74
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:15,000
I mean, they are responsible for the troop program.

75
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
They're responsible for meetings and outings.

76
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
They're responsible for the whole thing.

77
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:24,000
If he's receiving a written plan and is not actually responsible for the program,

78
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
he's responsible for running your idea of what the program should be.

79
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Let him own it. Let him come up with some ideas,

80
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Let him put them together, Let him run them.

81
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:40,000
He'll do a lot better than you think.

82
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
The other one is: never criticize your senior patrol leader publicly.

83
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Don't holler at him. Don't criticize him in front of other scouts.

84
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:52,000
No other action is going to undercut his authority

85
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
or the relationship that you form with him than doing that.

86
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Number three: give him some direction and some boundaries.

87
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Keep him focused on what scouting promises.

88
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Show him where the boundaries are,

89
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:13,000
But at the same time you do that, point out the vast possibilities

90
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:16,000
that exist within those boundaries.

91
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Number four: be polite.

92
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:26,000
I'm not polite. sometimes I have to say and ask permission

93
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,000
If he's running something, if he's leading the scouts and something,

94
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,000
don't just barge in, Ask permission.

95
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Hey, can I take a moment to say something?

96
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Ask permission

97
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Instead of jumping in and taking over and doing things.

98
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:48,000
hey, do you need any adult support for that?

99
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Or what can I do to help you do this

100
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Quietly, not in front of the other scouts.

101
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Be polite.

102
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:01,000
Number five is to encourage him.

103
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:04,000
There's always going to be problems and challenge is good.

104
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:10,000
He's responsible not to prevent the problems but to lead the troop through them

105
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:15,000
And problems, even if he creates them. they're not punishable offenses,

106
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,000
They're good moments to learn.

107
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
Number six: set the proper tone for leadership.

108
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:25,000
Scouts aren't soldiers. you're not the captain,

109
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:27,000
The SPL is not the drill sergeant.

110
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:31,000
You're the coach. your senior patrol leader is the team captain

111
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,000
and the scouts are the players.

112
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:36,000
Find out where the markings are on the field.

113
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,000
stay on the sidelines. let the SPL run the game.

114
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
Number seven is: use your authority sparingly.

115
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
First of all, you don't have a whole lot of authority anyway.

116
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:50,000
It's a good idea just to think that

117
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:53,000
We have provisional authority where we can step in

118
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
and we can prevent unsafe or improper actions.

119
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:00,000
But the real authority lies in our youth leadership.

120
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
When circumstances demand, do this quietly.

121
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Like I said, go back and ask permission

122
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,000
And see if you can quietly ask a lot of questions.

123
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
that will lead to a course correction.

124
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Don't grab the wheel out of their hands.

125
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Don't hit the brake unless the wreck is imminent.

126
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Ask some questions and guide them towards a course correction.

127
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
Number eight: keep your distance.

128
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,000
Keep your distance.

129
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
Let the scouts live their own lives.

130
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
I mean: go fold a tent in the storeroom,

131
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
Go conduct a scout master's conference,

132
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:45,000
Go sit down, have a cup of coffee,

133
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
Watch quietly.

134
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Go talk to your fellow leaders,

135
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
Stay out of their way,

136
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Keep your distance.

137
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Observe,

138
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
Observe, but remember. it's their place,

139
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
It's what they get to do.

140
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
It's their playing field,

141
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
And number nine follows up on that,

142
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
because we guard that playing field.

143
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
So we're not just coaches,

144
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
Sometimes we're referees,

145
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:14,000
And when other adults interfere

146
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:17,000
we've got to redirect their attention to something else.

147
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Usually one direct, clear statement about the role of adults

148
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
is all that you need to do,

149
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:26,000
And don't embarrass them, Be polite.

150
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
But sometimes the ref has to step in

151
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
and remind a coach that he's on the field now

152
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
and he needs to. you know,

153
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
hightail it for the sidelines.

154
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Number 10.

155
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
Always give your senior patrol leader plenty of feedback,

156
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:45,000
and plenty means about two minutes in every hour.

157
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:48,000
Don't wear them out.

158
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:51,000
Don't give him so much that you know

159
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
he can't process it.

160
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:57,000
Give him plenty of feedback about whatever's happening right. then

161
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Do it very, very quickly, quietly and politely

162
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:02,000
And then step back,

163
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Let him process it, Let him figure it out.

164
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:08,000
That's 10 little hints

165
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
that have helped me deal with my senior patrol leaders.

166
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
And you know, do I hit all 10 of them?

167
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Not always. Nope,

168
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
But we keep pushing ahead and we keep trying

169
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
And I think it'll help you sort out

170
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,000
how to work out this whole youth leadership puzzle.

171
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
Hi, I'm Kevin Callan and I'm listening to

172
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
Scoutmaster iPod. No, I can't.

173
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
What is it called? again,

174
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
I am Kevin Callan, the happy camper,

175
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:51,000
and you're listening to the Scoutmaster podcast.

176
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
Ah, beautiful.

177
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
Why do boys drop out of scouting?

178
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Well, the BSA several years ago

179
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
did a kind of an exit survey

180
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
with boys that had dropped out of scouting

181
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
and this is what they found out.

182
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
It's pretty simple. What do you usually hear?

183
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
What do you usually hear going around and things like that?

184
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
Well, there's too many other things

185
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
that compete with scouting, like sports

186
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
and stuff like that, and they go and do those.

187
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
Or, you know, the family didn't have

188
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
a real commitment to scouting,

189
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
or the boy didn't have a real commitment to it

190
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and when push came to shove, he got shoved,

191
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
You know. but the real reason,

192
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
the major conclusion

193
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
of this survey,

194
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
was that most boys dropped out of scouting

195
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
because they didn't get to go camping enough.

196
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
I've got a pretty simple theory about

197
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
why boys join scouting and I think you can back me up on this.

198
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
They join scouting because they want to go camping,

199
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
And they want to go camping

200
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and do this fun stuff with their buddies,

201
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
And when they don't get to do that,

202
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
they lose interest.

203
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Them playing on a baseball team

204
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
or something like that- well, that looks

205
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
a whole lot better because you get to do things

206
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
You know- just sit in meetings and listen to people talk-

207
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
And it's program

208
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
and relationships are the two major problems

209
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
and the two major causes

210
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
related to boys

211
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
dropping out of scouting.

212
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
When they're not having fun

213
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
they don't want to devote any time to the program.

214
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
They drop out so they can have more time

215
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
to do other things that they like to do more.

216
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
23%

217
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
of the boys that were surveyed

218
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
left scouting because they had conflict with the unit leaders.

219
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
That's almost

220
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
a quarter of the ones that go.

221
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
They left scouting

222
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
because they were in conflict with the unit leaders.

223
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Now, units with

224
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
a really good camping program-

225
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
ones that go camping once a month-

226
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
are a lot less likely to have those kind of conflicts.

227
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
69% of the boys

228
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
that left scouting

229
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
were the rank of second class

230
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
or less.

231
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Now, advancement

232
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
is the byproduct of a good camping program.

233
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
Most scouts

234
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
in units that camp monthly

235
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
can definitely rise to the rank of first class

236
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
within the first year that they're in

237
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
scouts. I don't think it's

238
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
worth programming them that way,

239
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
but they definitely have the opportunity to do that.

240
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
71% of drop scouts

241
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
came from troops who went on

242
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
eight or fewer outings a year.

243
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
Only 15%

244
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
of scouts that dropped out

245
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
camped every month.

246
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
51%

247
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
of scouts dropped out

248
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
because their troop had five or fewer

249
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
outings a year.

250
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
So this is pretty easy:

251
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
Go camping every month

252
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
and you have a statistical average

253
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
of very few boys

254
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
wanting to drop out of scouting,

255
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
according to this survey.

256
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
